{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789902480,"nonce":"n2HTA+pWAaqZFxNZPxtoRcoE4K8=","title":"Tiered: a lazy stub reached through a pointer spawns a compile thread on every call","message":"The stub standing in for a function compiled on first call spawns a 16 MB thread and joins it before looking at whether the function is compiled, so a code pointer taken before the entry was published (a Lua closure handed to table.sort, a metamethod) paid a thread per call: 2000 sorts of 20 elements took 4.7 s, 4.6 of them system time, and a comparator ran at 30 us a call with the warm-up thread disabled. Alongside, every compile_typed_program after the first spawned a new warm-up thread and left the previous one waiting on its queue for the life of the process: a program calling load in a loop accumulated one per load.","files":null}]}